# What must hold for each violation or diagnostic to go away: a goal names the predicate, and java.rules says how Java
# is, and so what makes it hold. Nothing here is a fix: repairs and quick fixes are deduced, and the verifier judges
# them. Whether a way keeps meaning is deduced too: one that breaks a promise of the language (a final, a field's
# static, a type) or makes up a value (a parameter its calls pass what its type starts with) is a quick fix people
# choose; only one that does neither may also repair a refactoring by itself.

goal access.insufficient needs accessible(site, target)
goal binding.unresolved needs in-scope(site, target)
# What Eclipse's compiler says does not resolve: a type, a variable or a field, a method.
goal ecj.UndefinedType needs resolves(symbol)
goal ecj.UndefinedName needs resolves(symbol)
goal ecj.UnresolvedVariable needs resolves(symbol)
goal ecj.UndefinedField needs resolves(symbol)
goal ecj.UndefinedMethod needs resolves(symbol)

# accessible(site, target): how Java says who can use what is in java.rules, and the planner makes it hold from there.

# in-scope(site, target): where a member can be used without saying whose is in java.rules.

# resolves(symbol) by a type of that name, or by a field or a parameter created for it: java.rules says it.

# A name misspelled: java.rules says it is changed to one almost it.

# A call to no method, a new no constructor makes, a type nothing declares: java.rules says what is created for each.

# A blank final field has its value where it is declared or from every constructor of its type: java.rules says it,
# and how each constructor takes the value and gives it.

# A constructor may not give a final field of its superclass its value: java.rules says how the superclass's
# constructor takes it instead (final-where), which keeps meaning, so a refactoring that pulls a field up plans it.

# A checked exception neither caught nor declared: java.rules says the callable it is thrown in declares it, which
# keeps meaning, so it also repairs a refactoring (statements extracted into a method).
goal ecj.UnhandledException needs declares-thrown(inside(path, line, column), first(arguments))

# A class that does not implement what its supertypes leave abstract: it implements each, or it is abstract too
# (java.rules says both, and when it may be abstract).

# A method used from a static place, where there is no object to call it on: java.rules says what needs an object.

# A private member nothing uses: java.rules says it goes.
goal ecj.UnusedPrivateField needs used-or-gone(symbol)
goal ecj.UnusedPrivateMethod needs used-or-gone(symbol)

# What a file cannot see: the member or type the name at the problem binds.
goal ecj.NotVisibleMethod needs accessible(top-in(path), element-at(path, line, column))
goal ecj.NotVisibleField needs accessible(top-in(path), element-at(path, line, column))
goal ecj.NotVisibleConstructor needs accessible(top-in(path), element-at(path, line, column))
goal ecj.NotVisibleType needs accessible(top-in(path), element-at(path, line, column))

# A value of another type than its place takes: java.rules says the place takes the value's type.

# A method that says it overrides and overrides nothing: java.rules says its superclass declares it.
goal ecj.MethodMustOverrideOrImplement needs overrides-one(symbol)

# A new object no constructor makes: the class gets one that takes what the new passes (java.rules says how).
goal ecj.UndefinedConstructor needs constructs(symbol)

# A final field assigned where it may not be: java.rules says who may (and making it not final is deduced).

# An override that hides what it overrides: it is as visible again, or more (java.rules says how).
goal ecj.MethodReducesVisibility needs visible-as-overridden(inside(path, line, column))

